-              <p>At \((c, c, c)\) every dealer earns 0. A deviation to \(c+1\) wins nothing from the others, who ask \(c\), so it earns 0 as well. The profile is an equilibrium. At \((c+1, c+1, c+1)\) each dealer earns \(Q/3\). A deviation to \(c\) earns 0, a deviation to \(c+2\) earns 0, and a deviation to \(c+1\) is a tie that earns \(Q/3\), which is no strict gain, so the profile is an equilibrium. At \((c+2, c+2, c+2)\) each dealer earns \(2Q/3\). The deviation to \(c+1\) wins all \(Q\) shares and earns \(Q > 2Q/3\), a strict gain, so the profile is not an equilibrium. This treats symmetric profiles only.</p>
+              <p>At \((c, c, c)\) every dealer earns 0. A deviation to \(c+1\) wins nothing from the others, who ask \(c\), so it earns 0 as well. The profile is an equilibrium. At \((c+1, c+1, c+1)\) each dealer earns \(N/3\). A deviation to \(c\) earns 0, a deviation to \(c+2\) earns 0, and a deviation to \(c+1\) is a tie that earns \(N/3\), which is no strict gain, so the profile is an equilibrium. At \((c+2, c+2, c+2)\) each dealer earns \(2N/3\). The deviation to \(c+1\) wins all \(N\) shares and earns \(N > 2N/3\), a strict gain, so the profile is not an equilibrium. This treats symmetric profiles only.</p>
